User James detailed a hydrological processing system designed to work with low-resolution weather data (0.1 degrees) and high-resolution topography (100 m), showcasing the HydroNet implementation in C++. Key challenges arose regarding memory efficiency and performance. The discussion highlighted several optimizations, including changing the passing of the river cache from value to reference to avoid excessive allocations, implementing checks to skip ocean cells in node generation, and simplifying the node structure to avoid complex subgraph representations. Recommendations included replacing `std::map` with more efficient data structures, ensuring proper memory management for created nodes, and avoiding duplicate rasters. The strongest advice was to develop a compact representation for equal-resolution scenarios instead of relying on the existing, memory-intensive structure.
